# Normalise every text file to LF in the working tree.
#
# This repository commits a built artifact (lib/) so the plugin can be
# installed straight from a git clone. That artifact must be byte-identical to
# what a rebuild produces on any machine, and two things break that on Windows
# with core.autocrlf=true:
#
#  1. git reports lib/client.js as modified on a clean checkout — same size,
#     same content, differing only in the EOL it would apply next checkout;
#  2. lib/client.js.map embeds the sources verbatim in `sourcesContent`, so a
#     CRLF working tree produces a map an LF checkout can never reproduce.
#
# Forcing LF everywhere makes the checkout — and therefore the artifact —
# platform-independent.
#
# ## Re-normalisation: checked, and not needed here
#
# A rule like this one does not rewrite blobs that are **already committed** — those keep
# whatever line endings they were stored with until a `git add --renormalize .` pass is run
# and committed. So the rule alone is not proof, and the review was right to ask.
#
# Measured on this repository: of all 142 tracked files, **zero** contain a CR in the index,
# and `git add --renormalize .` stages nothing beyond the working-tree edits already present.
# There is nothing to fix, so no renormalisation commit is required. If a CRLF blob ever does
# land (an editor writing one under a rule this file does not cover, say), the check is:
#
#   git add --renormalize . && git diff --cached --name-only   # empty means clean
#
# ## The one exception the blanket rule would otherwise swallow
#
# `eol=lf` applies to *every* text file, including the few types that genuinely need CRLF on
# Windows. None exist in this repository today; the carve-out is here so that adding one is not
# silently broken by this file, and so the constraint is visible rather than rediscovered.
#
# These come **after** the blanket rule on purpose: for the same attribute, the last matching
# pattern wins, so putting them above it would have them overridden by the very rule they exist
# to except. (`git check-attr eol -- scripts/foo.ps1` is how to confirm the order.)

* text=auto eol=lf

*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Binary formats; keep git from inspecting or converting them.
#
# The list is explicit rather than "media", because `text=auto` falls back to git's NUL-byte
# heuristic for anything unlisted — and that heuristic is not guaranteed for every binary
# format, so an unlisted one can be mangled by EOL normalisation. Extend this list when a new
# binary type lands rather than assuming the heuristic covers it.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.eot binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.tgz binary
*.pdf binary
*.wasm binary
*.node binary
